A||N||1||turbo86se||Indy Pace Car Story......||||7||
Z||000000||turbo86se||12-04-2000||08:48 PM||||Hey guys and ladies...<BR>I just thought I'd share this story with ya'll, it's from the June '97 issue of The DixieLand Fieros newsletter. "I Wish I Had a V8", Mr. P-Body's Corner.     I hope you enjoy it,<BR>     "Have you ever experienced a time when a car turned your head twice?  This experience happened to me.  It was 5 AM as I was cruising to work in my '86GT or 'scooter' as my 9 year old daughter named it. You know as well as I that when you see another Fiero, you just have to check it out. So I was checking my rearviewmirror and spotted a white Indy Pace Car gaining on me. It's after market wheels and lowered stance grabbed my attention. I gave him a thumbs up as he passed by. My neck popped again as I noticed the massive air cleaner sticking three inches out of the hood, and the two mufflers protruding out of the area the exhaust tips normally reside. He stepped on the accelerator, the car squatted down, and disappeared effortlessly in traffic. I was mad that I couldn't take chase because of work. I had heard stories of this car from the local salvage yards but had never seen it. I had been told that a local diesel mechanic had shoehorned a small block Chevy in a Fiero and that he worked for Coburg Dairy. The Indy Fiero stuck in my mind all day and I decided to find it. I found the address for Coburg Dairy and sped over to find the owner before he left work. There it was in front of the fleet garage. Its stance was menacing. I parked my car in front of his and waited. The owner who introduced himself as Shane finally came out. I made a brief introduction and begged to see his car. I expected to see a V8 Archie or Zumalt kit installed, instead I found a Cadillac trans-axle mated to a small-block Chevy, normally aspirated, and heavily massaged. I know what you're going to say 'how can he cut up a Pace Car', but he was quick to point out that the car was a 200$ basket case when he started. Being a man on a budget, he found the V8 kits rather expensive. So he used his mechanical abilities to adapt the Caddy combination to the car. He solved the problem of having lots of horsepower and being able to get further than the next gas pump. The four speed overdrive can cruise at 80 mph with the engine turning a mere 1500 rpm. It is also beefy enough to sustain heaps of torque at the local drag strip where the Fiero is a formidable contender. And yes it is a daily driver. Although I find his installation to be superb, he has told me that it was his first attempt and that he could make better changes on his next attempt." <P>||||reg||7
Z||000001||MrPBody||12-04-2000||11:06 PM||||Just for the record, that's a different Mr. P-Body.<BR>||||reg||1
